Overview
This experiment studies the impact of simultaneous traffic within a LAN on overall network performance, with emphasis on delay and packet loss under varying load conditions.

Objective
To generate multiple ping requests at the same time in order to analyze queueing behavior.
To compare how the network performs under light traffic versus heavy traffic conditions.
To understand how congestion occurs in shared links and why Quality of Service (QoS) mechanisms are necessary.

Methodology
Network Setup:
A LAN topology was created consisting of a single switch connected to six PCs. Each device was assigned a static IP address to maintain proper communication.
Initial Test (Low Traffic):
A single ping stream was sent from PC1 to PC6 to observe network performance under minimal load.
Stress Test (High Traffic):
Three ping operations were executed simultaneously (PC1→PC6, PC2→PC5, PC3→PC4) to create congestion on the shared network link.

Observations
(Replace with your own findings if needed)
In the low traffic scenario, the network showed stable performance with minimal delay and no packet loss.
When multiple ping requests were generated simultaneously, the response time increased, indicating higher latency.
A few packets were dropped during high traffic, showing the effect of congestion.
The results demonstrate that limited bandwidth in shared links can lead to bottlenecks.
This experiment highlights the importance of implementing QoS techniques to manage traffic efficiently.

Files Included
